  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}+0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3}\\in U$ and the zero-polynomial $q(x)=0\\in U.$ Then \n\n$$\np(x)-q(x)=p(x)-0={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\nq(x)-p(x)=0-p(x)=-{x}^{3}\\ne {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
